  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 135 PM CDT Tue Jul 28 2026\n\nA very subtle wave continues to move to the southeast. As it does so \nclouds will dissipate, gradually becoming scattered to clear. \nSimilar to yesterday, smoke from wildfires will result in an overall \nmilky appearance to the sky. Highs today will warm into the upper \n80s to low 90s. Dew points will be lower than the past couple of \ndays but still in the mid 60s to low 70s, highest over northwest \nIowa. Localized higher values are possible in areas with large corn \nfields, as the plants add moisture through evapotranspiration. Heat \nIndex values remain below critical today. However, it is still \nimportant to take care of yourself while working in hot conditions. \nWinds today will be southerly and light. Overnight they become light \nand variable. Patchy areas of fog are possible as overnight lows \nfall to the mid 60s and low 70s, which is right around the dew point \ntemperature. Most of the fog should be contained to water ways and \nlow areas, and will burn off by mid-morning Wednesday.\n\nNorthwest flow aloft continues for Wednesday, keeping the smokey \nskies in place. Wednesday during the day will be very similar to \ntoday with highs in the upper 80s and low 90s. Southerly winds \nincrease in the afternoon as a shortwave approaches from the west. \nGusts of 15-25 mph are expected with gusts increasing as you move \ntoward central South Dakota. Showers and thunderstorms form in the \nlate afternoon and evening over western South Dakota and progress \neast. Storms will reach central South Dakota and our western CWA \nborder around midnight. There remains some variance in mid-\nrange guidance on how this wave and resulting storms will \nevolve, therefore uncertainty in severity remains high. Model \nsoundings show a dry subcloud layer and tall skinny CAPE. This \nindicates the most likely threats will be strong to marginally \nsevere wind gusts and periodic heavy rainfall. A weak LLJ helps \nto maintain convection through much of the day Thursday. \n\nMeanwhile, an upper level trough develops over the Pacific Northwest \nand speeds east. The mid-level and upper troughs deepen and a \nsurface low forms over eastern Wyoming and western South Dakota. \nSimilar to Wednesday night's storms, uncertainty in storm evolution \nis high due to wide variance in guidance. In addition, the \nactivity and outflows from storms earlier in the day will likely\nplay a role in evening development. The most likely scenario is\na few isolated strong to severe supercells form over central \nSouth Dakota and quickly grow upscale into an MCS with damaging \nwind gusts and periodic heavy rain the primary threat. This \ntracks east through the overnight and early Friday morning, \nbringing beneficial rainfall to much of the area. LREF \nprobabilities of 0.50 inches of rain or more are 55-75% region \nwide. This probability is for total rainfall from Wednesday \nnight through Friday morning. \n\nThursday will be cooler due to clouds and rain with highs in the low \nto mid 80s. Friday continued CAA will result in highs slightly \ncooler in the upper 70s to low 80s. A beautiful weekend is in store \nwith mostly sunny skies and highs right around average for this time \nyear in the mid 80s.",
